    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data regarding thermal management in high-performance braking systems. Specifically, I'm comparing the heat dissipation rates of carbon-ceramic discs versus semi-metallic pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles. Can you explain the primary mechanisms of heat soak in these materials and how they contribute to brake fade during heavy use?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In carbon-ceramic brake systems, heat soak primarily occurs when the material absorbs more heat than it can dissipate quickly, raising temperatures until it approaches a critical phase transition temperature, weakening the disc. Semi-metallic pads, on the other hand, consist of metallic fibers bonded with a ceramic matrix. The metals in the pads have high thermal conductivity, helping dissipate heat more effectively, thus reducing the rate of heat soak. However, during heavy use, excessive heat can still accumulate, causing brake fade in both materials as the friction coefficient decreases and braking efficiency drops off.",
      "source": "target",
