    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm particularly concerned about the inconsistency in the semi-metallic pads during long-duration testing. Sometimes the thermal profile is stable, but then it starts acting up—unpredictable spikes in temperature that don't seem to align with the deceleration force applied. Is there a mechanical or chemical reason for that kind of erratic behavior in the friction material?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "These erratic spikes in temperature you're observing with semi-metallic pads can indeed be attributed to several mechanical and chemical factors. On a mechanical level, inconsistencies in the pad composition, such as varying distribution of metallic fibers, can lead to uneven heating. Areas with fewer fibers will retain heat more, potentially causing localized hotspots. Chemically, the build-up of wear debris at the pad surface can disrupt the lubrication between the pad and the rotor, resulting in higher localized temperatures due to increased friction. Additionally, microscopic fractures in the pad can expose fresh metal surfaces, exacerbating degradation and leaving the brake shoe more susceptible to temperature spikes during use.",
      "source": "target",
